            "text": "{\"strengths\":[\"Clear causal chain: lower confidence leads households to postpone consumption and save more, reducing aggregate demand.\",\"Accurate, well-integrated AD–AS diagram showing AD shifting left against unchanged SRAS, with real output and the price level both falling.\",\"Strong explanation of firms’ inventory response and output adjustment; the answer correctly distinguishes a demand contraction from a fall in productive capacity.\"],\"improvements\":[\"Make the short-run assumption explicit at the start of the diagram explanation: SRAS is held constant while other components of AD are assumed unchanged.\",\"Optional extension (not needed for credit): briefly connect the multiplier mechanism more explicitly to the further reduction in household spending.\"] ,\"mistakes\":[],\"examinerComment\":\"Estimated practice mark: this is a highly focused and accurate response. It explains the consumption-to-AD mechanism, uses a clear and fully relevant AD–AS diagram, and traces the movement to lower equilibrium real output and price level. The qualification about SRAS responsiveness and offsetting AD components is accurate and does not distract from the core explanation.\",\"studyNext\":\"Optional extension (not needed for credit): practise explaining how the slope of SRAS affects the relative size of the real-output and price-level changes after an AD shift.\",\"assessmentFormat\":\"paper_1_a\",\"paper\":\"paper_1\",\"questionPart\":\"a\",\"levelRelevance\":\"shared_sl_hl\",\"assessmentSkills\":[\"diagram_explanation\",\"economic_analysis\"],\"commandTerm\":\"explain\",\"commandTermLabel\":\"Explain\",\"syllabusUnit\":\"unit_3\",\"syllabusTopic\":\"3.2\",\"topicLabel\":\"AD/AS variations\",\"classificationConfidence\":\"high\",\"markingConfidence\":\"high\",\"diagramExpected\":true,\"diagramSubmitted\":true,\"diagramAssessmentStatus\":\"submitted_and_assessed\",\"workingsExpected\":false,\"workingsSubmitted\":false,\"workingsAssessmentStatus\":\"not_relevant\",\"attachmentContent\":\"diagram\",\"assessableEarned\":10,\"markBreakdown\":[{\"label\":\"Knowledge and terminology\",\"awarded\":4,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"Consumer confidence, consumption, aggregate demand, SRAS, real output and price level are used accurately.\"},{\"label\":\"Economic analysis\",\"awarded\":4,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"The response develops a coherent chain from weaker confidence and reduced consumption through lower AD, inventories, lower production and a new short-run equilibrium.\"},{\"label\":\"Diagram\",\"awarded\":4,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"The submitted AD–AS diagram clearly shows AD1 shifting left to AD2 against SRAS, with equilibrium moving from E1 to E2 and both PL and real GDP falling.\"},{\"label\":\"Structure and clarity\",\"awarded\":4,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"The answer is logically sequenced, directly addresses both requested outcomes, and uses the example and qualifications to reinforce rather than replace analysis.\"}],\"bandRationale\":\"Best fit: 9–10. The response is fully focused and analytically developed, with an accurate, effective required AD–AS diagram integrated into the explanation. It establishes both the reduction in real output and the fall in the price level, without material conceptual omissions.\",\"limitations\":[],\"examinerJudgment\":{\"task\":\"Explain how a fall in consumer confidence may affect real output and the price level.\",\"demonstrated\":\"Accurate explanation that lower confidence reduces planned consumption, shifts AD left, creates unwanted inventories and leads firms to reduce production; a clear AD–AS diagram establishes lower equilibrium output and price level.\",\"materialLimitations\":[],\"selectedBand\":\"9-10\",\"withinBand\":\"upper\",\"diagramEffect\":\"integrated\",\"summary\":\"A complete, coherent and accurate explanation with an effective necessary diagram; the response securely meets the top-band characteristics.\"},\"componentEvaluation\":null}"
          }
